4.47.m:
"This swarm of ants was viewed by me one by one in succession.
All of them, by their own karma, O Śakra, became Indras in the abode of the gods.
Now, through the karma of their past births in succession,
in the course of time they have attained birth as the creature ant.
Through karma living beings go to healthy Vaikuṇṭha;
through karma to Brahmaloka, and to Śivaloka through karma;
to heaven, to an assembly equal to heaven, and to Pātāla through their own karma.
Through karma [they go] to dread hell, the sole cause of suffering for the self;
through karma into the womb of a sow; through karma into the life of lowly creatures;
[into the wombs] of animal females through karma; of avian females through karma;
through karma into the womb of an insect, and to the state of a tree through their own karma.
Through one's own karma one is happy or sorrowful, a master or a servant;
through karma one attains Brahminhood, and divinity too through one's own karma.
Through one's own karma one becomes a ghost, and through one's own karma attains Brahma-hood.
Through karma one is afflicted with disease, and only through karma is one exceedingly handsome.
